=== clean | a bare slash is only a policy question under the uqf profile
x:1
/ a comment

=== QP002 profile: uqf | the legacy datetime type
t:2024.01.01T00:00:00.000

=== clean profile: uqf | a timestamp has the precision uqf wants
t:2024.01.01D00:00:00.000000000

=== clean profile: uqf | an indented slash is a line comment, not a block comment
  /
a:1
f:{x+
 /
 1}

=== QP001 profile: uqf | a terminated block comment is still policy, not syntax
/
text
\

=== QP003 profile: uqf | the wall-clock timestamp where UTC is the convention
t:.z.P

=== clean profile: uqf | UTC temporals are what the profile asks for
t:.z.p

=== QP005 profile: uqf | multiplication mixed with addition relies on evaluation order
r:2*3+4

=== clean profile: uqf | parenthesised, the order is said out loud
r:(2*3)+4

=== QP005 profile: uqf | in a statement that ends in a semicolon, in a one-line lambda and in brackets (#511)
f:{[a;b;c]
  r:a*b+c;
  r}
g:{[a;b;c] a*b+c}
h:2*3+4;
k:(2*3+4)
m:f[2*3+4;1;2]

=== clean profile: uqf | each bracket is an expression of its own, so (a*b)+c mixes nothing
f:{[a;b;c] r:(a*b)+c; r}
g:f[(2*3)+4;1;2]

=== clean profile: uqf | a single operator family is unambiguous
r:2*3*4

=== clean profile: uqf | a minus sign after a space is a negative literal, not subtraction
a:"p"$1234567890123456j*2 3 0N 0w -0w 5 -.5

=== QP005 profile: uqf | a minus between operands, spaced or not, is subtraction
r:2*b - 1

=== clean | dynamic evaluation turns checks off without that being a defect
v:value "x"

=== clean | a load is dynamic too
\l utils.q

=== clean | eval is dynamic
r:eval parse "1+1"

=== clean | a load through system is dynamic
system "l utils.q"

=== clean | value on a dictionary reads it, nothing is evaluated
r:value `a`b!1 2

=== clean | value on a lambda decomposes it
r:value {x+1}

=== clean | system with a shell command defines no names
system "mkdir -p out"

=== clean | set with a symbol literal is a definition the file spells out, and the global is known
`cfg set 1
f:{[a] a+cfg}

=== QF014 | the local-scope rule runs even where the file evaluates dynamically
v:value "x"
f:{[x] r:cfg; cfg:1; r}

=== clean | dynamic evaluation still skips the undefined-global check that would say 'x
v:value "x"
f:{[a] a+x}

=== QF010 | without it, the same lambda draws the check
f:{[a] a+x}

=== QF010 | a quote in a comment after value is not a string it evaluates
d:`a`b!1 2
r:value d / the "values"
f:{[a] a+x}

=== QP001 QE003 profile: uqf | a bare slash at column 0 opens a block comment, and nothing closes it
a:1
/

=== clean profile: uqf | nothing after a lone backslash is read, a bare slash included (q 5: b is 'b)
a:1
\
/
b:2

=== QP001 profile: uqf | while a backslash that closes a block comment does not end the script
/
text
\
/
more
\

=== clean profile: uqf | an indented slash does not: q keeps reading code after it
a:1
  /

=== QP006 profile: uqf | a function this file defines, applied without brackets
f:{x+1}
r:f 2

=== QP006 profile: uqf | a call straight after select (#780; q 5: select g s from t is g[s])
g:{x+1}
t:([]s:1 2;k:`a`b)
r:select g s from t

=== QP006 profile: uqf | and after exec (#780; q 5: exec g s from t is 2 3)
g:{x+1}
t:([]s:1 2;k:`a`b)
r:exec g s from t

=== QP006 profile: uqf | and as the right operand of an infix keyword (#780; q 5: where s in g 1 filters on g[1])
g:{x+1}
t:([]s:1 2;k:`a`b)
r:select from t where s in g 1

=== QP006 profile: uqf | and as the table after from, which keeps its finding with no fix (#780; q 5: select from g t applies g)
g:{x}
t:([]s:1 2)
r:select from g t

=== clean profile: uqf | a column named like the function, before from, by or where, is not a call (#780)
g:{x+1}
t:([]s:1 2;g:3 4)
a:select g from t
b:select s by g from t
c:select from t where g in 1

=== clean profile: uqf | outside a query `where` is a unary, whose argument this rule leaves alone as it does `sum f x` (#780)
g:{x>1}
b:1 2 3
r:b where g b

=== QP006 profile: uqf | an hsym path argument whose `/` is part of the symbol, not over (#388; q 5: f `:db/t is f[`:db/t])
f:{count x}
n:f `:db/t

=== clean profile: uqf | the name inside an hsym or a symbol with a colon is part of the symbol (#400; q 5: `:f 0: x is (0:;,`:f;`x))
f:{x}
`:f 0: x
r:`a:f 1

=== clean profile: uqf | a name before the I/O verb 0:, 1: or 2: is its left operand, not applied (#508; q 5: parse "fmt 0: x" is (0:;`fmt;`x), and f[1:`a] does not parse)
fmt:{x}
w:fmt 0: ("a";"b")
v:fmt 1: 0x0102
u:fmt 2:00

=== clean profile: uqf | the same call with the brackets the convention asks for
f:{x+1}
r:f[2]

=== clean | juxtaposition is only a policy question under the uqf profile
f:{x+1}
r:f 2

=== clean profile: uqf | a builtin reads better without brackets, and is not this rule's business
f:{x+1}
r:count "abc"

=== clean profile: uqf | a name this file never defines as a lambda might be a dictionary or a list
r:lookup `a

=== clean profile: uqf | a name assigned something other than a lambda is indexed, not called
t:`a`b!1 2
r:t `a

=== QP006 profile: uqf | a namespaced function, and the argument runs to the end of the expression
.ns.scale:{x*2}
r:.ns.scale 3+4

=== clean profile: uqf | an adverb applies to the function, it does not call it
f:{x+1}
r:f each 1 2 3

=== clean profile: uqf | an infix builtin puts the function on the left of an operator
f:{x+1}
r:f in `a`b

=== clean profile: uqf | a local of the same name says nothing about the global
f:{x+1}
g:{[f] f 2}

=== clean profile: uqf | a system command's argument is not an argument
d:{x+1}
\d .ns

=== QP006 profile: uqf | a call at the head of its own line
step:{x+1}
step 1

